 - **Small, frequent integration (Merijn, 2026-10-16 evening: "we work agile / DevOps; small, frequent commits are better").** One-piece flow for code: the coordinator gates and pushes **each agent commit on its own as it arrives**, never a wave of several; agents merge origin before **every** commit, not only the last; a red gate stops the line for that commit only (it goes back to its owner), and the rest keeps flowing. Why: on 2026-10-16 evening a two-hour unpushed batch in the gate tree turned into three cross-agent conflicts and two red gates.
 - "Send now" in the app interrupts every running agent and a stopped agent cannot resume: relaunch it fresh, pointed at its worktree.
 - ✅ **The Godot suite no longer OOMs (2026-10-17).** Two leaks, measured with `SPANNING_TEST_MEM=1`: (1) the main one, a reference cycle since 760a214 (2026-10-16 evening): `GameSession.new_game` gave `Missions.talked` a lambda capturing the session, so no `GameSession` (with its whole region and network, ~10 MB) was ever freed: one process reached 6.7 GB. Now a bound method, as `talk_outcome` already was; `test_a_dropped_session_is_freed` guards it (rule: a lambda stored on an object the session owns must not capture the session). (2) `run_tests.gd` ran every test inside `_initialize`, so no frame passed and nothing the game `queue_free`s (`UiPanel.clear`, the creator booth's models) was deleted until quit: 26 000 orphan nodes after `test_creator_screen.gd`. The runner now lets a frame pass after each test (`test_runner.gd` guards it), and `tools/run_godot_tests.py` runs each file in its own process (2 at a time, slowest first from the last run's times in `game/.godot/test_times.json`), fails on any file that died without a summary, and is the gate and CI line. `SPANNING_TEST_MEM=1` prints memory per file.
-- ⬜ **Speed: `test_dialog_routes.gd` takes ~10 minutes alone** (the width-300 beam search over 20 tier-1 talks), the critical path of the parallel run; next `test_save_slots.gd`, `test_session.gd`, `test_office_scene.gd` (2–3 min each). Lower the beam width in the test or cache the talks.
+- ✅ **Speed: `test_dialog_routes.gd` 590 s → ~196 s (2026-10-17), same results.** Profiled: the beam sort scored every node on every comparison, `pick()` built the whole move palette per propose, `copy()` ran the constructor's start work, and T11 repeated the full search of the test before it. A dump of every asserted result (tier-1 best route 9/20 and its talks, each content talk's winners, distinct routes and winnability, the tutorials' wins without their move) is identical before and after. Tried and dropped: worker threads across talks (no gain on a loaded 4-core machine) and a faster `state_key` (within noise). Left: the full search of the 29 content talks (~2 min).
+- ✅ **Speed: the next slowest files (2026-10-17).** Profiled per test (`SPANNING_TEST_TIMES=1`): the six-month warm-up was ~2 s of every `GameSession.new_game`; it now runs once per content, seed and pull limit and each game gets a `NetworkState.copy` (~50 ms), byte-identical (six games' full snapshots plus 30 more days of play compared). `test_save_slots` 193 → 35 s, `test_session_dialogs` 73 → 11 s, `test_session_layer` 54 → 15 s, `test_session` ~140 → ~60 s alone; the whole suite 2043 → 822 s under load. Left as they are, the cost being the thing tested: `test_catalog` (83 s of it is `test_an_expert_can_solve_every_problem`, one Check forecast per problem on its own region), `test_session`'s fade forecast (18 s), `test_office_scene` (~40 s: ~1 s per office instance, each test mutates its own).
 
